 The companion numerical script evaluates the harmonic scales, the finite-energy saturation identity, the dimensionless dispersion, the covariance determinant, and the quench-amplitude limit. It generates only derived values and analytic test cases. No generated row is labeled as an experimental image, an observed spectrum, or a newly fitted temperature.
+
+Dedicated checks compare Equation~\eqref{eq:expanded_dispersion} with the factorized dispersion and test convergence to Equation~\eqref{eq:relativistic} as both dimensionless kinetic energy and coherent coupling decrease. Printed-value consistency and arithmetic sanity checks are counted separately from analytic identities and model benchmarks; the direct cancellation of a common factor in $a/\lop$ is not an independent test of carrier non-identifiability.
 
 The executable numerical supplement is \path{analysis/reproduce.py}, requiring Python 3.10 or later and only the standard library. Running \texttt{python analysis/reproduce.py} regenerates three files in the \path{analysis/} directory: nominal results in \path{nominal_scales.json}, the harmonic sweep in \path{confinement_family.csv}, and the named test report in \path{analytic_checks.json}. The named checks also cover the scaled readout-to-gap ratio, the conditional transverse thermal benchmark, mean-field balance at $Z_0$, common-profile and unequal-profile overlaps, the feasible confinement scaling, and the finite-pulse bound in explicitly declared toy models. They test analytic identities and numerical implementation, not experimental profile equality or source-level closure.
 
